---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Source caveat, stated on the page itself: the published Nature Biotechnology article is not open
// access (Unpaywall: is_oa false, no PMC record), so this summary is written against the bioRxiv
// preprint v5 (10.1101/092890, posted 9 January 2018), which is the fullest legally available text.
// The previous version quoted F1 figures (0.9972 / 0.9854 / 0.9780 / 0.9938), an allele-fraction
// threshold of 0.12 and an AVX-512 requirement, none of which appear in that text.
// Checked by `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug deepvariant`.
const title = "A Universal SNP and Small-Indel Variant Caller Using Deep Neural Networks";
const description = "Technical summary of DeepVariant (Poplin et al., Google Brain & Verily, Nature Biotechnology 2018): recasting variant calling as image classification over read pileups, with a model that generalizes across sequencing technologies, genome builds and species.";
const readingTime = "20 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Variant Calling & Deep Learning</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Nature Biotechnology · 2018 · read here via preprint v5</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Ryan Poplin, Dan Newburger, Jojo Dijamco, Nam Nguyen, Dion Loy, Sam S. Gross, Cory Y. McLean, Mark A. DePristo</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> Google Brain (now Google Health) & Verily Life Sciences, Mountain View, CA</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/nbt.4235" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1038/nbt.4235</a> · Nature Biotechnology 36, 983–987 · preprint <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/092890" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1101/092890</a></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      Conventional variant callers are stacks of hand-built statistical models — GATK alone uses logistic
      regression for base errors, hidden Markov models for read likelihoods and naive Bayes for genotyping —
      and each new sequencing technology needs the stack retuned by hand. <strong>DeepVariant</strong>
      replaces the whole assortment with one convolutional network that reads <strong>pileup images</strong>
      and emits diploid genotype likelihoods. Trained on one human sample and evaluated on an unseen one, it
      reaches <strong>SNP F1 = 99.95% and indel F1 = 98.98%</strong>, won the <strong>"Highest SNP
      Performance" award at the FDA-sponsored precisionFDA Truth Challenge</strong>, and generalizes across
      genome builds, sequencing platforms and even <strong>to mouse</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The Retuning Problem</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">2. Architecture: Variant Calling as Image Classification</a></li>
        <li><a href="#accuracy">3. Accuracy on Held-Out Human Samples</a></li>
        <li><a href="#generalization">4. Generalization: Builds, Platforms, Species</a></li>
        <li><a href="#technologies">5. Where the Candidate Callset Breaks Down</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">6. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#honesty">7. The Paper's Own Statement of What It Got Wrong</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & Reading This Summary</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The Retuning Problem</h2>
      <p>
        A variant caller has to separate differences between reads and the reference that reflect real genetic
        variation from those that reflect sequencing error. The established approach models the error process
        explicitly — and the model is specific to the instrument that produced the errors.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper names the consequence: generalizing these models to other sequencing technologies
        <strong>has proven difficult due to the need for manual retuning or extending these statistical
        models</strong>, which it calls <strong>a major problem in an area with such rapid technological
        progress</strong>. Every new platform costs another round of expert modelling.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. Architecture: Variant Calling as Image Classification</h2>

      <h3>Two stages</h3>
      <p>
        DeepVariant begins by <strong>finding candidate SNPs and indels in aligned reads with high sensitivity
        but low specificity</strong> — deliberately over-calling, so that the neural network's job is to reject
        rather than to discover. The deep model then classifies each candidate.
      </p>

      <h3>The pileup image</h3>
      <p>
        Around each candidate variant, the reference and read data are rendered as a
        <strong>three-channel image</strong> of the read pileup. The network — <strong>Inception-v2</strong>,
        an off-the-shelf image classification architecture — <strong>emits probabilities for each of the three
        diploid genotypes</strong> at the locus.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Why the image representation is the idea
        </div>
        <p>
          Rendering reads as an image sounds like a gimmick and is not. Everything a human expert looks at
          when inspecting a questionable call in a genome browser — allele balance, strand bias, base
          qualities, mapping qualities, the local pattern of mismatches — is present in the visual field, and
          spatially arranged. A convolutional network is built for exactly that kind of local structure, and
          it learns which combinations matter <em>from data</em> rather than from a modeller writing them
          down one platform at a time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="accuracy" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. Accuracy on Held-Out Human Samples</h2>
      <p>
        The evaluation is constructed to prevent the model from memorising a sample. DeepVariant was trained
        on data from the <strong>CEPH female sample NA12878</strong> and evaluated on the <strong>unseen
        Ashkenazi male sample NA24385</strong>, achieving <strong>SNP F1 = 99.95% and indel F1 = 98.98%</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same dataset and evaluation methodology were then applied to a range of established tools —
        <strong>GATK, FreeBayes, samtools, 16GT and Strelka</strong>. DeepVariant
        <strong>outperformed all other methods for calling both SNP and indel mutations</strong>, and did so
        without the per-tool tuning those methods normally require.
      </p>
      <p>
        Independently, variant calls for the blinded sample NA24385 were submitted to the FDA-sponsored
        <strong>Truth Challenge in May 2016</strong>, where DeepVariant won the
        <strong>"highest performance" award for SNPs</strong> — judged by an independent team using a
        different evaluation methodology, which is the strongest form the result could take.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="generalization" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Generalization: Builds, Platforms, Species</h2>

      <h3>Across genome builds</h3>
      <p>
        A model trained on one genome build can be applied to other versions with <strong>effectively no loss
        in accuracy</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Across species — the striking result</h3>
      <p>
        A model learned from <strong>human reads and human ground-truth data</strong> achieves
        <strong>F1 = 98.29% on a mouse dataset</strong>, <strong>out-performing a model trained on the mouse
        data itself (F1 = 97.84%)</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> Why human-trained beats mouse-trained on mouse
        </div>
        <p>
          The paper calls this experiment <strong>especially demanding</strong>: not only do the species
          differ, but nearly all of the sequence differs too. That the human-trained model still wins says
          what the network actually learned — not human genetics, but the <em>error process of the
          instrument</em> and how it looks in a pileup. That is species-independent, and it is why non-human
          sequencing projects can <strong>benefit from the wealth of human ground-truth data</strong> they
          could never generate themselves.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Across experimental designs</h3>
      <p>
        The same approach learns to call variants across designs ranging from <strong>deep whole genomes from
        10X Genomics to Ion Ampliseq exomes</strong> — the paper's claim being that, unlike existing tools
        which perform well on only a specific technology, one architecture covers the range.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="technologies" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Where the Candidate Callset Breaks Down</h2>
      <p>
        The analysis of non-Illumina data is the most useful part of the paper for anyone deploying this, and
        it is about the <em>first</em> stage rather than the network.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>SOLiD and PacBio</strong> WGS datasets have <strong>high error rates in the candidate
        callsets</strong>. For SOLiD, the candidate positive predictive value is <strong>13.9% for SNPs, 96.2%
        for indels, and 14.3% overall</strong> — the SNP artifacts arising from mapping short, colour-space
        reads.
      </p>
      <p>
        A 13.9% PPV means roughly six in seven candidate SNPs are wrong before the network sees them. The
        network's job on that platform is overwhelmingly rejection, and the achievable ceiling is set by
        whether the true variants survive candidate generation at all — no amount of classifier quality
        recovers a variant that was never proposed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Mathematical Formulation</h2>
      <p>
        For a candidate site, let <Katex tex="I" /> be the rendered pileup image. The network outputs a
        distribution over the three diploid genotype states — homozygous reference, heterozygous, homozygous
        alternate:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="P(G \mid I) = \operatorname{softmax}\big(f_{\theta}(I)\big), \qquad G \in \{0/0,\; 0/1,\; 1/1\}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        The final call is the maximum-likelihood genotype, with the reported quality derived from the
        probability assigned to the reference state:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\widehat{G} = \arg\max_{G} P(G \mid I), \qquad \text{QUAL} = -10 \log_{10} P(G = 0/0 \mid I)" />
      </div>
      <p>
        What is notable is the absence of the usual machinery. There is no explicit error model, no HMM over
        read alignments, no hand-specified feature set and no separate variant-quality recalibration step —
        the likelihood that those components exist to compute is produced directly by
        <Katex tex="f_{\theta}" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="honesty" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. The Paper's Own Statement of What It Got Wrong</h2>
      <p>
        The manuscript contains an unusually direct limitations section, and it is worth quoting the substance
        rather than paraphrasing it away.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, on the representation: using <strong>general image-classification models is
        "certainly suboptimal"</strong>, because the authors were <strong>unable to effectively encode all of
        the available information in the reads and reference into the three-channel image</strong>. The image
        is a lossy interface to the data, chosen because it let them use an existing architecture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, on the software: the manuscript <strong>documents the work leading up to the precisionFDA
        Truth Challenge in May 2016</strong>, and since then DeepVariant has been
        <strong>entirely rewritten from scratch</strong> to be released as open-source software — so
        <strong>several improvements to the method are not captured in the analyses presented here</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> What this means for reading the numbers
        </div>
        <p>
          The accuracy figures above describe the 2016-era system. The DeepVariant anyone installs today is a
          different program, and its published benchmarks are correspondingly better. Treat this paper as the
          argument for the <em>approach</em> — learned representations replacing hand-tuned error models — and
          consult current release notes for the numbers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & Reading This Summary</h2>

      <h3>Scope of the method</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SNPs and small indels only.</strong> Structural variants, large indels and copy-number changes are outside the candidate-generation step entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Bounded by candidate generation.</strong> Recall is capped by the high-sensitivity first stage; the SOLiD result shows how badly that stage can degrade on an unfamiliar platform.</li>
        <li><strong>Ground truth is the ceiling.</strong> Training and evaluation rest on Genome in a Bottle truth sets, which are themselves least reliable in the repetitive and structurally complex regions where calling is hardest — so measured accuracy is optimistic exactly where it matters most.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>A note on this page's source</h3>
      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          The published Nature Biotechnology article is <strong>not open access</strong> — Unpaywall reports
          no OA location and there is no PMC record — so this summary is written against the
          <strong>bioRxiv preprint, version 5, posted 9 January 2018</strong>, which is the fullest legally
          available text and carries the preprint title <em>"Creating a universal SNP and small indel variant
          caller with deep neural networks."</em> Figures quoted above are the preprint's. The published paper
          may report revised values, and where this page could not verify a number against a source it does
          not state one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
